> We can have a look at adaptively reducing the allocation.

In practice, could this be even more efficient and "quick win" with #2548 or even, as a first step, by taking into account an existing PRIMARY KEY constraint?
In my usage all aggregations are performed on tables that are already naturally sorted (timeseries with timestamp column as primary key) and noticed the same ballooning memory usage.

In the simple testcase below, using ORDER BY on primary key takes 25x longer (+ allocations).
Perhaps sorting could be completely avoided in such cases and this should also greatly benefit aggregations (and thus related allocations) perhaps? 

```sql
CREATE TABLE series (i INT64 NOT NULL, PRIMARY KEY (i));
INSERT INTO series SELECT * FROM generate_series(10000000);

SELECT * FROM series;
-- Run Time (s): real 0.007 user 0.026780 sys 0.008383

SELECT * FROM series ORDER BY i;
-- Run Time (s): real 0.180 user 0.810616 sys 0.245748
```
